  "worked_solution": "First, let's factor $p$ from the expression. We have\n\n$$\n9p+p{t}^{2}=p(9+{t}^{2}).\n$$\n\nLet's now focus on factoring the expression $(9+{t}^{2}).$\n\nTo factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9+{t}^{2} & =9-(-{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =9-({\\text{i}}^{2}{t}^{2}) \\\\  & ={3}^{2}-(\\text{i}t)^{2} \\\\  & =(3+\\text{i}t)(3-\\text{i}t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our main expression, we get\n\n$$\np(9+{t}^{2})=p(3+\\text{i}t)(3-\\text{i}t).\n$$",
